# Which test suites use the shared framework, and which still carry a case
# runner of their own.
#
# tests/testlib.ps is the one place a case that may raise is run, and the
# one place that puts the world back afterwards. A suite asks for it with
# `%!testlib` on its first line; run-ps-test.sh prepends it to those and to
# nothing else, because some suites here assert that a program's own
# dictionary starts empty or count the dictionary stack, and six read their
# own source through currentfile.
#
# This file is the list of suites that have NOT moved, with the shapes each
# one's own runner takes -- which is what a conversion has to preserve if
# the move is to be checked by diffing the suite's output rather than by
# reading it. The list is held to the tree in BOTH directions: a suite that
# grows its own runner and is not named here fails, and a name here whose
# suite no longer has one fails too. So the list can only shrink, and it
# shrinks by conversion rather than by editing.
#
# The guard also refuses the one mistake that would undo all of this: a
# suite that asks for the framework and then defines one of its names
# anyway. That shadows the shared runner with a local one silently, which
# is exactly the arrangement that let a false pass sit in a hundred and
# forty-six files.
#
# TWO DISPOSITIONS, and the second is why this file is a register rather
# than a to-do list. `own` is a suite that has not moved yet. `cannot` is
# one that must not move, and carries the reason: a suite whose subject is
# the state of a program's own dictionary cannot be handed a framework
# defined into that dictionary, because the framework is then what it
# reports. Without somewhere to say that, the only records of it would be
# a conversion that failed and whoever next tried the same thing.
#
# HOW A CONVERSION IS CHECKED, and what the check does not cover. Every
# suite moved here was run through the harness before and after and required
# to print the same bytes. That is a strong check on behaviour and no check
# at all on strength: a suite whose own runner fixed the expected error name
# inside itself, converted to a shared runner that accepts any error, goes
# on printing SUCCESS while asking a weaker question. So a conversion that
# changes which question a case asks must also show the case failing when
# the answer is wrong.
#
# The prose above ends at the two legend lines below, and the guard checks
# they are still there. A rewrite of this file that generates the register
# by finding the first `own` in it truncates the header at the word in
# `own source through currentfile` and merges the first entry onto the
# comment, which nothing reading only the uncommented lines can see.
#
#   own    <suite>  <runner>[ <runner>...]
#   cannot <suite>  <runner>[ <runner>...]  <reason>

own    op_radix_test.ps               outcome

cannot startup_surface_test.ps        raises  it asserts that a program's own dictionary starts empty, which is the first thing it checks and the reason it exists; a framework defined into that dictionary is exactly what it would report. MEASURED: converted, it fails on "userdict starts empty" and on nothing else

owns 1
cannots 1
